WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
Film Courage: How is being an artist&nbsp;
very different from being the audience?

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:08.960
Glenn Gers, Screenwriter: That is one of the&nbsp;
hardest things to get used to because we all begin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.680
as an audience. I don't think there's an artist&nbsp;
in the world who didn't start by being a fan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.800
We all want to do these things because&nbsp;
we liked seeing them, absorbing them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:28.640
we were the best consumers and the truth is the&nbsp;
artist never experiences their own work the way an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:34.960
audience will because they first you know what's&nbsp;
going to happen. You can't actually experience it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:40.240
you have to reverse engineer that whole experience&nbsp;
just like if you were building a roller coaster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:45.520
You can't be riding the roller coaster at the same&nbsp;
time. You have to actually say I need a support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.800
here and I need a dip here and you're being very&nbsp;
mechanical and frankly artificial about it because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:57.760
you're saying I want them to get a throw when&nbsp;
they go over that hump and in order to do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.600
I’m going to have to have this amount of force&nbsp;
up here and then it's going to have to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.440
tailing out at that point and it's going to become&nbsp;
very mechanical in order to give somebody else the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:15.280
pleasure of riding that roller coaster. That's&nbsp;
what you need to do as an artist is to say I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:21.440
the experience of going over that hump and then&nbsp;
zooming down and for some reason I don't crash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:27.680
at the bottom that's because someone knew how to&nbsp;
get out of the freefall that I’m going through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:34.960
artist has to stop and learn the mechanics how you&nbsp;
support the track that's going to do that how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:41.920
make the story twist and turn and provoke those&nbsp;
experiences that you yourself will never have and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:48.320
when you're actually doing it you get this weird&nbsp;
kind of duality because as you're creating you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.040
usually have like a little tiny bit of an&nbsp;
audience's experience you'll be like I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.480
actually enjoying the line I just thought of as&nbsp;
an audience did you'll have that for a fraction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.360
of a second while you're doing it and then after&nbsp;
that you have to figure out the mechanics to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:10.640
that scene work so you're constantly going back&nbsp;
and forth sort of saying I want to be an audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.480
now and now in order to get that audience&nbsp;
I got to go back and become the mechanic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:19.200
and you're constantly cycling through them&nbsp;
you can never really give up being an audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:25.840
but you have to also become the person building&nbsp;
the ride the other thing about artists and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:34.080
audiences is that from the finished product you&nbsp;
can never tell how it got made one of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:41.120
I think artists really should do is try to read&nbsp;
or hear podcasts or books about how things you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:46.960
love got made because you'll always be amazed&nbsp;
at how the people who set out to do it did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:52.720
know that the final result was going to be what we&nbsp;
now think of as inevitable and the the stories are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:58.320
actually I just read one about midnight cowboy&nbsp;
and and when they brought the book Midnight Cowboy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:02.560
John Schlesinger the director and the producer&nbsp;
brought the book to MGM The studio and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:10.160
said we want to do this very dark story about this&nbsp;
wannabe hustler going to Times Square and MGM said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.160
we think this could be an Elvis Presley vehicle&nbsp;
and we're going to put in some songs and we'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:22.640
take out that dark stuff so you know on a bad day&nbsp;
MGM could have taken it over and it could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:28.240
been Elvis Presley in Midnight Cowboy which would&nbsp;
have been something to see but the point is when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:35.600
we watch midnight cowboy it never occurs to us how&nbsp;
many different things had to go right or had to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:42.560
learned to make that happen and having worked&nbsp;
behind the scenes having created things I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:49.120
tell you that there are things that I did not know&nbsp;
going in somebody brought it to me sometimes whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:56.080
story ideas were not my idea in fact the script&nbsp;
I'm best known for fracture wasn't my original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:01.520
script someone else had written a very, very good&nbsp;
script the studio wanted to make changes someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:08.000
else was hired after that then I was brought in&nbsp;
so many of the plot twists I didn't come up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.960
I did a lot of the writing that made those plot&nbsp;
twists work in that form that it did end up in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.040
but when you watch that movie you come to me&nbsp;
and say how did you think of that plot twist the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:23.920
answer is I read it in somebody else's script and&nbsp;
I used it because that's the job so the answer is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:31.280
you can't just look at stuff you love and go oh I&nbsp;
know how they did that you'll never know how they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:37.200
did that and you'll never know how your work&nbsp;
is going to come out you just don't you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:45.200
to get used to the experience of trying to get&nbsp;
something done doing the best you can and seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:52.960
how it plays you had talked about something in the&nbsp;
artistic process abstract to concrete to abstract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.680 --> 00:05:00.720
yes what is that that's sort of a follow-up&nbsp;
of the artist audience issue which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:08.960
you nothing will ever come out like it feels to&nbsp;
you that is part of the art I don't I don't think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:13.520
there's any artist who hasn't gotten hopefully&nbsp;
used to it because it's part of learning how to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:19.600
it one of the things that brought this to me was&nbsp;
I was once looking at a Picasso drawing and late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:24.160
Picasso drawings especially are just like they're&nbsp;
like scribbles you know it's it looks sort of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:31.120
a bull but how did he know to make the line go&nbsp;
like how did he know to do that because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:38.560
just a scribble but it's a bull and I suddenly&nbsp;
realized he didn't know he knew that whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:45.680
came out would be interesting he trusted that and&nbsp;
that's what every artist has to learn that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:52.320
don't know exactly how it's going to come out you&nbsp;
can outline I outline incredibly thoroughly and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:56.320
think I know what's going to happen but the&nbsp;
truth is that there's going to be a process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:03.840
after that the creative process good word for&nbsp;
it you're creating something out of nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:10.080
and when that happens it's going to be something&nbsp;
that you're going to discover as you do it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.240
then you need to shape it and rework it and do&nbsp;
all the other things that artists have to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:21.360
but the thing in your head there's this feeling&nbsp;
or this idea or this line and then you put it on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:27.120
the page and then somebody else performs it and&nbsp;
then someone else sees it and in all of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:33.280
steps it's not what you thought it was going to be&nbsp;
and the best thing an artist can do is get really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:39.040
comfortable in a sense getting comfortable&nbsp;
with a failure you're going to fail to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:45.600
your exact feeling and thought out to someone else&nbsp;
that's that's life it will never ever happen that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:51.920
someone gets exactly what you thought it was but&nbsp;
what will happen is you'll get used to doing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:58.240
scribble and someone will go oh cool bowl and&nbsp;
that's art that's the magic of it is that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:03.040
get used to bringing something abstract into&nbsp;
a concrete form and someone else gets an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:08.640
abstract experience out of it that's that's&nbsp;
something that only art does it's beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:16.880
so get used to sounds like just experimenting and&nbsp;
and trusting that I may not know where it's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:22.480
but this kind of feels right and knowing that&nbsp;
that same thing you could see it could be changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:28.240
drastically and you have to be collaborative and&nbsp;
okay with that get used to the process get used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:34.000
the experience of doing the thing over and over if&nbsp;
you're playing tennis you get used to hitting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:39.600
ball you hit it over and over and then eventually&nbsp;
you'll get to where you can control it more but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:44.160
still there's going to be a lot of other elements&nbsp;
and what only thing that you can get used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:50.880
is the process of making the swing likewise the&nbsp;
process of thinking of something writing it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:57.840
a concrete form editing it shaping it rewriting&nbsp;
it giving it to other people that process that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.320
what you have to practice that's what you have&nbsp;
to get to enjoy and feel comfort in and the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:05.520
that you do it the more that you'll get lucky&nbsp;
and things will work out and you'll be like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:09.840
oh I think this line is funny and then you write&nbsp;
it down and someone reads it and they laugh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:15.440
that gives you so much power for the next&nbsp;
time you do it because sometimes it's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:25.840
to work and sometimes it's going to not work&nbsp;
but the more you do it the more it will work

